{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And he commanded, etc., to all his people, etc.</b> According to what we have written, that at first he spoke to the midwives so that the matter would be in secret and the daughters of Israel would not know, when that did not succeed in his hand he did the thing publicly and commanded all his people concerning the matter, for he thought that there would be no escape from them, since they were numerous and recognized and would not have pity on them. Further, perhaps it is because he saw through his astrologers that the savior of Israel had been born and was destined to be stricken by water (Sanhedrin 101a), therefore he issued a harsh decree and assigned every Egyptian man to the matter, and through this there would be no escape from their hand." "And His saying <b>leimor</b> — meaning, it is not that the Egyptians themselves would cast him, but rather that they would say to the children of Israel that each one must cast his own sons. And the reason for what we said, that the decree was because of what their astrologers saw, etc., can be inferred from the obscurity: they would perceive the manner of the blow that struck him — that it was through Israel — therefore he decreed that the children of Israel themselves would cast him into the river, and so it was, that Yocheved herself cast Moses into the river." "<b>Or</b> it may intend <b>leimor</b>, that they would command every neighbor in his neighborhood concerning the matter, and would not need all the people." "And there are among our Sages who said (Sotah 12a) that he also decreed upon the Egyptians, and this is the way of midrash — that on a single day, when they told Pharaoh, for on that day, etc., on that day he decreed upon all his people. But according to the plain sense of the verse, the matter is stated only concerning Israel, for this decree was continuous, and the proof is that Yocheved placed Moses in the river after three months, and the astrologers had seen the day of birth, and had the decree been only on the day of Moses's birth and that day was recognized, what room would there be to decree further? Rather, it is certain that this decree concerning every male born was continuous, and necessarily His saying <b>to all his people</b> means to command them concerning the children of Israel — and the midrashic reading shall be read as midrash."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:35.927334097Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}